[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix queued. Its reports describe three problems.
Each now has its own ticket, and each needs its own fix.

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued parser fix closes one ticket only. Do not close #4471's reports as fixed when it ships.
</Callout>

## The three problems

<TicketSplit ticket={4471} links={{ parser: `${repo}/1`, hint: `${repo}/2`, shift: `${repo}/3` }} />

## How to tell them apart

- **Parser.** The report came by app or email, or through a booking-v2 form, from a tenant that
  writes the day first. Day and month swap: 7 April becomes 4 July.
- **Hint.** The report came through the form of a booking-v3 tenant. The form prints `MM/DD/YYYY`,
  people type month first, and the form reads the entry the tenant's way. The text looks the same as
  a parser report, so only the channel and the tenant's form separate them. A parser fix does not
  change this form.
- **One-day shift.** The date moves by one day: Tuesday 12 May shows as Monday 11 May. No swap of day
  and month does that. One report has the same meeting on different days in Lisbon and Toronto, so
  look at time zones first. That cause is not confirmed.

## Do not count from these

- **The intake tag.** It follows the reporter's browser, not the tenant. Every report tagged `hint`
  came from an `en-US` browser, wherever the tenant is and whichever form it runs, so the tag misses
  hint reports and marks parser reports as hint. The desk's table below shows both errors.
- **The raw export.** Its pages overlap, so some reports appear twice. Count by id.
- **Subject lines.** [Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) treats a matching subject
  as the same issue. In these reports, the same subject appears under different problems, and one
  problem has many subjects.

## The desk's table

This is the table on #4471. It files the one-day reports under `parser` by tenant and footnotes them.
The ticket table above counts them on their own. Its "no ticket of its own" text means the state
before these tickets.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## What is not known

The cause of the one-day shift is a lead, not a finding. One of its reports, the invite that arrived
before the booking, could also be a flip. The export ends on 4 October and the desk still takes
reports, so the counts here move when the data does.

